Act as a senior full-stack web designer, UX/UI strategist, and creative art director.

Create a refined promotional website concept for **vitrify**, a production journal for professional ceramic ateliers.

The website should present vitrify as a calm, precise, serious tool for ceramic studios that need to track physical pieces, production states, firings, failures, stock, students, classes, clients, and orders over time.

Core title:
Production journal for ceramic ateliers.

Core idea:
vitrify records the physical life of ceramic work: what exists, where it is in production, why it exists, who it belongs to, what entered the kiln, what came out, what failed, what is ready, what is reserved, and what the atelier can learn from that history.

vitrify helps professional ceramic studios track physical pieces through production states and firings, while organizing stock, class work, student pieces, client orders, custom orders, tests, and external pieces. It replaces scattered notebooks, spreadsheets, messages, habits, memory, and shelf-based tracking with a traceable production record.

The product is not ecommerce, not CRM software, not a kiln controller, and not an app for students. It is a production memory system for ateliers.

Positioning:
Ceramic work is physical, cumulative, slow-moving, and easy to lose track of. Pieces wait on shelves, enter firings in batches, come back changed, fail unpredictably, get remade, become reserved, belong to a student, sit ready for a client, or disappear into studio memory.

vitrify makes this work traceable.

The site should explain the daily value:
Knowing what needs attention now.

And the long-term value:
Knowing what the atelier has learned over time.

The record becomes more useful with every firing.

Key questions the website should surface:
- Which student pieces still need firing?
- Which custom-order pieces are blocked by failure?
- What is actually available stock?
- Which pieces are reserved, ready, delivered, or missing?
- What failed in the last firing?
- What needs to be remade for the next fair?
- How many bisque mugs are left for Friday classes?
- Which external pieces are waiting in the kiln queue?
- How many pieces broke last month?
- Which glaze colors sell the most?
- Which forms fail most often?
- Which kiln loads produce the most loss?
- Which stock is ready but not moving?
- What is the atelier still carrying in memory instead of recording?

Product explanation:
vitrify starts from the object itself.

A piece is one real physical object. If five identical mugs are made, they are five pieces, even when the interface groups them visually.

Each piece can carry:
- production state
- purpose
- firing history
- failure history
- value
- notes
- stock status
- student connection
- class or workshop context
- client or order connection

Failure should not be treated as a normal production stage. It should be recorded as an outcome: visible, traceable, and useful for history, statistics, loss analysis, and remakes.

Core features to communicate:
- Record physical pieces
- Track production states
- Load and complete firings
- Record failures and losses
- Derive stock from real objects
- Link pieces to students, classes, clients, and orders
- Preserve atelier history over time
- Export and import data
- Connect to a Shopify webstore
- Replace Excel and Google Sheets
- Allow late recording and correction without interrupting the studio day

Production contexts:
Show that stock is only one part of the atelier. The same studio may manage general production, workshop pieces, student work, brought-in pieces, glaze tests, custom client orders, replacements after failure, and one-off experiments — all moving through the same shelves and kilns.

Include these piece contexts:
1. Stock  
   General production pieces available, reserved, sold, delivered, or missing.

2. Student work  
   Pieces linked to a student, whether made in a class, workshop, or independent studio context.

3. Class or workshop piece  
   Pieces created during a session, grouped by class context while remaining individual physical objects.

4. Custom order  
   Pieces reserved for a client or order, with production state, failures, replacements, and completion status.

5. Client order  
   Pieces linked to a person or client context without turning the system into CRM software.

6. Test or experiment  
   Glaze tests, form trials, samples, one-offs, and uncertain work worth remembering.

7. External or brought-in piece  
   Work brought into the studio by a student or client, especially when it enters the kiln queue.

Statistics philosophy:
Do not frame vitrify as a dashboard for the sake of a dashboard.

Statistics should emerge naturally from the ordinary production record:
- pieces made
- pieces fired
- pieces failed
- pieces sold
- stock movement
- recurring losses
- kiln outcomes
- student and class output
- remakes
- custom-order problems

The goal is not vanity metrics. The goal is better studio memory and better operational decisions.

